.TH LEXSPELL 1 "October 2026" "nylex 0.2"
.SH NAME
lexspell \- the spelling program of nylex, for Swedish and English
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lexspell
[
.B \-d
.I dictionary
] [
.B \-u
.I userlist
] [
.B \-c
.I commonlist
] [
.B \-k
.BR swe7 | latin1 | utf8
]
.I function
[
.I document
[
.I output
] ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Lexspell
checks the spelling of
.IR nylex (1)
documents, looks words up and keeps word lists.
It is called from
.IR nylex 's
spelling menu as LEX called its own dictionary program: a record of
the system file names the program (characters 1-32), the dictionary
(33-64), the user's word list (65-96), the common word list (97-128)
and the function (161; when it is blank, the first character of
keystore 0).
The menu switch options /D (a document), /H (a document and another
for the output) and /N (nothing asked) run it;
.I nylex
passes the record in
.BR $LEXREC ,
keystore 0 in
.B $LEXKS0
and the terminal's character code in
.BR $LEXCS .
In
.IR nylex 's
own system files the records are
.BR *=SPLDEFC ,
.BR *=SPLDEFA
and so on, one for each function.
.SS Functions
.TP
.B C
Check a document.
Each word not in the dictionary nor in the word lists is shown in its
line with suggestions: a number takes a suggestion, R types a
correction, Return or A accepts the word for the rest of the document,
L adds it to the user's list, F finishes (the changes so far are kept)
and Q abandons the check (the document is left as it was).
With the Swedish dictionary the letters are Swedish: R, G (godta),
E (egen ordlista), A (avsluta), Z.
The document is saved, the old one as
.IR name .bak.
Attribute lines (underline, bold ...) are kept in step.
.TP
.B A
The same, with an extra word list as well: the file named in keystore
0 after the function letter, or asked for.
.TP
.B E
Extract the unknown words of a document into another, one a line,
sorted.
.TP
.B S
Spell words: each word typed is right, or the suggestions are shown.
.TP
.B M
Find the words that fit a pattern: ? any letter, * any letters
(crosswords: d??k*).
.TP
.B L
List the dictionary from a word on, a screen at a time (F finishes).
.TP
.B W
Add words to the common word list.
.TP
.B F
Add the words of a file to the common list.
.TP
.B X
Take words out: they go into the common list as words not to accept.
.TP
.B Y
The same for the words of a file.
.PP
A word is right if it is in the dictionary, in the user's word list or
in the common one.
In Swedish a word not in the dictionary is right as well when it is a
compound of parts that may begin, go in the middle of and end one
(\c
.IR husvagnsd\(:ack ,
.IR krokodilsko ),
with a triple consonant written as two (\c
.IR tunn\(oal ).
The suggestions are words with a letter changed, left out, added or
two swapped, the word in other capitals, and the word split in two.
.PP
The messages are in Swedish or English, after the dictionary.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-d " dictionary"
.B sv
(Swedish, the default),
.B en-GB
(British English; also
.BR en )
or
.B en-US
(American English), the files
.IB name .dict
in the library; or a file named with a /.
.TP
.BI \-u " userlist" ", \-c " commonlist
the word lists (default
.B $HOME/user.def
and
.BR $HOME/common.def ):
text files, a word a line; "-word" in the common list is a word not to
accept.
.TP
.B \-k swe7\fR|\fBlatin1\fR|\fButf8
the character code of the terminal and of the word lists typed (as
.IR nylex 's
.BR \-c ).
Documents are in 7-bit Swedish, as LEX keeps them.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
LEXREC, LEXKS0, LEXCS as above; LEXLIB, the library (instead of the
one built in).
.SH FILES
.TP
/usr/local/share/nylex/sv.dict, en-GB.dict, en-US.dict
the dictionaries (on the D-NIX Amiga port in /usr/modern/lib/nylex)
.TP
$HOME/user.def, $HOME/common.def
the word lists
.SH SEE ALSO
nylex(1).
.SH NOTES
The dictionaries are made by
.B tools/mkdict.py
of the Hunspell dictionaries that LibreOffice uses: every form the affix
rules give, with flags for the parts of compounds, front-coded in blocks
of 64 words with an index (832,630 Swedish words in 4.3 MB, 266,531
British English, 123,689 American English).
The Swedish dictionary is G\(:oran Andersson's (LGPL 3), the English ones
are from SCOWL and the LibreOffice project (LGPL, and a permissive
licence for en-US); see dict/README.
LEX-68's own dictionary programs and dictionary are not used.
